# Mobile test layout

| Kind | Path | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Flow, one for each scenario | `test/mobile/<feature>/<nn>-<scenario>.flow.yaml` | `test/mobile/sign-in/01-valid-account.flow.yaml` |
| Plan | `test/mobile/plan/<feature>.plan.md` | `test/mobile/plan/sign-in.plan.md` |
| Subflow, the start steps of a plan | `test/mobile/subflows/<name>.yaml` | `test/mobile/subflows/open-sign-in.yaml` |
| Suite file, optional | `test/mobile/config.yaml` | `test/mobile/config.yaml` |

## Flow rules

- One flow file serves Android and iOS. Never write one file for each platform. A step that must differ goes in a `runFlow` block with `when:` and `platform: Android` or `platform: iOS`.
- Line 3 of a flow is `appId: ${APP_ID}`. The run command gives the app id.
- The first command of a flow is `launchApp` with `clearState: true` and `clearKeychain: true`. No flow needs another flow to run first.
- Select an element by `id:` first. Use its whole visible text only when it has no id. Never use `point:` or other screen coordinates.
- A check of an element that has an id gives `id:` and `text:`, so the text is checked too. Leave `text:` out only for a text that changes from run to run, such as an order number.
- Write `- hideKeyboard` between typing and the next tap, and nowhere else. With the keyboard open, a tap can close the keyboard and miss the button. With no keyboard open, `- hideKeyboard` presses back on Android.
- There is no command that waits a fixed time. `tapOn` and `assertVisible` wait up to 17 seconds by themselves. For a slower screen use `extendedWaitUntil` with `timeout: 60000`.
- A product bug is marked with the tag `fixme`, and the line above `- fixme` is a comment that names the source line: `# product bug: src/screens/SignIn.tsx:41 expected "Wrong email or password", got "Login failed"`. Never tag a flow `fixme` for another reason.

## Run a feature's folder

`RTK_DISABLED=1 maestro test --platform android --exclude-tags=fixme -e APP_ID=com.example.app test/mobile/sign-in`

Change only the platform word (`android` or `ios`), the app id, and the folder. The generate job uses this command. The plan and heal jobs do not: they use the maestro MCP server. A `maestro test` run removes Maestro's helper app from the device when it ends, and the MCP server then cannot reach the device until it is turned off and on.

The user starts the device and installs the app. Never start a device, and never switch to the other platform.
